WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:05.806
..because that is when,
unfortunately...

00:00:07.808 --> 00:00:09.443
Species A is worth 50 bucks

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:11.645
and species B
is worth $10,000.

00:00:16.717 --> 00:00:20.187
Biodiversity is declining
in such a rapid rate.

00:00:24.658 --> 00:00:25.759
I find that terrifying.

00:00:41.408 --> 00:00:43.744
But what is driving the biggest
changes today is...

00:00:50.284 --> 00:00:52.152
One solution to this problem...

00:00:54.555 --> 00:00:56.156
...because, unfortunately,
in the world we live in...

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.129
Can these very tools

00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:03.864
that drive
the decline of nature

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:06.633
be the tools that help us
reverse the process?

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:10.304
Humans emerged from nature.
We're part of the biosphere.

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:19.346
It is really important for our
physical and mental wellbeing.

00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:21.181
I think it's useful
to think about an ecosystem,

00:01:21.215 --> 00:01:23.784
and whether an ecosystem
destroyed or harvested

00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:26.587
is worth more or less
than an ecosystem intact.

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:29.890
In many cases, there is more
value in that ecosystem and...

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.629
One of the really big issues,
I think,

00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:38.398
with valuing nature is,
how do you do it?

00:01:42.703 --> 00:01:45.506
When you cut down a tree
and you sell it as timber,

00:01:45.539 --> 00:01:47.207
you're able to realise

00:01:47.241 --> 00:01:49.109
the market value
of that timber.

00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:51.745
Whereas if that tree
still remains in a forest,

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:54.615
it's playing a lot
of functional roles,

00:01:54.648 --> 00:01:57.217
for surrounding communities
and even the global community.

00:01:57.251 --> 00:01:58.685
Look at these lovely plants!

00:01:58.719 --> 00:02:00.787
One really interesting question
is...

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:06.660
The question has
an obvious answer.

00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:09.696
The Amazon rainforest is
the centre of biodiversity

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:12.466
on planet Earth -
there's no way that is worth

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:14.268
less than a company.

00:02:21.208 --> 00:02:23.477
So if we started, for example,
by looking at the carbon value

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:24.811
in terms of climate change
mitigation,

00:02:24.845 --> 00:02:26.747
the rainfall
that's being generated

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:27.915
by that rainforest existing,

00:02:27.948 --> 00:02:30.551
the biodiversity that provides
lots of interactions

00:02:30.584 --> 00:02:33.420
around pollination
and controlling pests,

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:36.523
that may one day provide
medical benefits for people.

00:02:36.557 --> 00:02:38.859
And then on top of that,
that rainforest is ancient.

00:02:38.892 --> 00:02:40.460
It's been around
for 60 million years.

00:02:40.494 --> 00:02:41.962
And that has intrinsic
respect and value

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:45.165
that we also need
to consider in the equation.

00:02:45.199 --> 00:02:46.834
Viewing nature as an asset,

00:02:46.867 --> 00:02:49.870
rather than a commodity,
is absolutely essential,

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:52.306
because an asset you always
want to build on,

00:02:52.339 --> 00:02:54.741
you want to enhance,
and you want to protect.

00:03:13.327 --> 00:03:14.761
One of the big arguments
is that

00:03:14.795 --> 00:03:18.899
it doesn't recognise
the intrinsic relationships

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:20.567
that people have with nature.

00:03:26.607 --> 00:03:29.676
And none of those can be
properly represented with...

00:03:31.745 --> 00:03:33.747
We cannot depend
upon pricing of nature

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.782
as the solution to problems
because....

00:03:38.318 --> 00:03:40.554
I mean, the danger
with putting a price on nature

00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:43.357
is that you create incentives
for people

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:46.393
to cheapen nature, because they
want to make money off of it.

00:03:46.426 --> 00:03:48.529
Communities that live
close to nature,

00:03:48.562 --> 00:03:50.397
and are dependent
on nature directly,

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:51.532
are often the poorest.

00:03:51.565 --> 00:03:53.534
As a consequence,
given their low incomes,

00:03:53.567 --> 00:03:56.904
the economic value
of what they get from nature,

00:03:56.937 --> 00:03:59.473
the positive benefits that they
get, turns out to be very low.

00:04:03.277 --> 00:04:05.579
..living close to nature,
but in poverty,

00:04:05.612 --> 00:04:07.414
and therefore eliminating
their voices

00:04:07.447 --> 00:04:09.349
from the decision-making
system.

00:04:15.889 --> 00:04:18.325
In the state of California,
if you're a developer,

00:04:18.358 --> 00:04:19.660
you want to build
a bunch of houses,

00:04:19.693 --> 00:04:21.628
you know that
your new housing complex

00:04:21.662 --> 00:04:25.766
is going to kill off breeding
pairs of burrowing owls.

00:04:25.799 --> 00:04:28.802
You need to account for those
burrowing owls someplace else.

00:04:28.836 --> 00:04:30.404
And so you have to then say,

00:04:30.437 --> 00:04:33.507
"I'm willing to pay $2,000
for a burrowing owl.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:34.842
Who out there can conserve

00:04:34.875 --> 00:04:36.610
a burrowing owl
for $2,000 for me?

00:04:36.643 --> 00:04:37.711
Because that's the budget
that I have."

00:04:44.084 --> 00:04:46.053
One of the pioneering examples

00:04:46.086 --> 00:04:48.322
of bringing nature
into decision-making

00:04:48.355 --> 00:04:49.423
was the country
of Costa Rica...

00:04:49.456 --> 00:04:51.558
Which has gone from
predominately deforestation

00:04:51.592 --> 00:04:53.827
to reforestation.

00:04:57.431 --> 00:04:59.466
So Costa Rica now,
by most metrics,

00:04:59.499 --> 00:05:01.068
has the highest
Human Development Index,

00:05:01.101 --> 00:05:02.903
is the most prosperous
and developed of the countries

00:05:02.936 --> 00:05:04.004
of its neighbourhood.

00:05:18.585 --> 00:05:20.387
...let alone the wellbeing
of nature

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:22.322
that could sustain
human wellbeing over time.

00:05:22.356 --> 00:05:24.324
Rethinking
the fundamental structure

00:05:24.358 --> 00:05:27.461
of what's a good economy,
a good nation, a good life

00:05:27.494 --> 00:05:28.929
is a fundamental part
of what we need to do.

00:05:28.962 --> 00:05:30.731
There's not one silver bullet
here,

00:05:30.764 --> 00:05:32.399
but I think this approach

00:05:32.432 --> 00:05:34.101
of valuing nature
is an important one.

00:05:35.135 --> 00:05:37.037
In some ways,
it is like that discussion

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:38.539
between idealists
and pragmatists.

00:05:38.572 --> 00:05:41.341
Some people feel we should not
be putting a price on nature,

00:05:41.375 --> 00:05:43.977
and ethically,
it's not right to do it.

00:05:44.011 --> 00:05:46.580
All of us have
that idealist part in us.

00:05:46.613 --> 00:05:49.516
But we also have to be
entirely pragmatic.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:51.118
We need a blueprint for nature

00:05:51.151 --> 00:05:52.619
that says
these parts of the globe

00:05:52.653 --> 00:05:53.887
are the most important
for nature,

00:05:53.921 --> 00:05:56.156
and we absolutely
must protect them.

00:05:56.356 --> 00:05:58.992
Putting a price on nature
is one way to value.

00:05:59.026 --> 00:06:02.362
It is not the only way
to value nature.

00:06:02.396 --> 00:06:05.465
And so if we just recognise
that monetary valuation

00:06:05.499 --> 00:06:09.503
is but one of a suite of ways,
that would go a long way.

